 Here, I'll find the difference in squares of the LHS and RHS, and then use difference in squares to factorise. Let $y = \frac{x}{2x-1}$. \[
\begin{split} \frac{x^2}{(2x-1)^2} & \le 9 \\ \frac{1}{(2x-1)^2} & = \left( 3 -\frac{1}{x} \right)\left( 3 + \frac{1}{x} \right) \\ \frac{1}{(2x-1)^2} & = 9 - \frac{1}{x^2} \\ \frac{x^2}{(2x-1)^2} & = \left( \frac{9x}{x^2-1} \right)\left( \frac{9x}{x^2-1} \right) \\ x^2 & = (2x-1)^2\left( 9x \right)^2 \end{split} 
\]</think> After dividing each side by $2x-1$, squaring and then canceling, we get $x = \frac{(2x-1)^2}{9}$. we complete the solution by solving for $x$. We simplify and factor to get $x = \frac{1}{3}, -1$. The final answer is $\boxed{\left\{-1,\frac{1}{3} \right\}} $ </answer>

